  "Question": "Solve \\(\\{x+y=3,\\ y+z=5,\\ x+z=4\\}\\). (Give the values of \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\), in this order, separated by commas.)",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "1，2，3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Adding the three equations gives \\(2(x+y+z)=12\\), so \\(x+y+z=6\\). Subtracting each equation in turn gives \\(z=6-3=3\\), \\(x=6-5=1\\), and \\(y=6-4=2\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Express y and z using x\n\nExpressing both \\(y\\) and \\(z\\) in terms of \\(x\\) reduces the remaining equation to one unknown. From \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+z=4\\), we have \\(y=3-x\\) and \\(z=4-x\\). Substituting into \\(y+z=5\\) gives\n\\[\n(3-x)+(4-x)=5,\n\\]\nso \\(7-2x=5\\) and \\(x=1\\). Then \\(y=3-1=2\\) and \\(z=4-1=3\\). The requested values are \\(1,2,3\\).",
